#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049119 :topic/difficulty 1.6353301804901486 :topic/id #uuid "dc19c9ce-6319-5943-8842-352e3c3735c7" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092240 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092083 :question/id #uuid "e405bf60-d500-55df-90be-a938dc9dfc93" :question/math-academy-id "e-6080" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=x^3 - x^2$ at the point $(1,0)$ is given by $y=px+q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the values of $p$ and $q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=x^3 - x^2.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}-{x}^{2})=3{x}^{2}-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1, 0){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=3-2=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line $m'$ is\n\n$$\nm'=-\\dfrac{1}{m}=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =-(x-1) \\\\ y & =-x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-1$ and $q=1.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a323ba-f2a7-5a49-abd4-a9812518ef8c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092384 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091953 :question/id #uuid "4a734f55-ceac-587c-a831-fa8bed578a99" :question/math-academy-id "e-148" :question/problem "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y = 2x^3 -x^2$ at the point $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x) = 2x^3-x^2.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\nf'(x)  =6x^2 -2x\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nm=f'(1) = 6(1)^2 - 2(1) = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line $m'$ is\n\n$$\nm' = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = -\\dfrac 1 4.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a05f6c0-98ab-5197-922e-8e8dff1d4302" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092385 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091954 :question/id #uuid "df59a07a-922c-50b9-8c2c-22ed57ac2bda" :question/math-academy-id "e-149" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x) = 2\\sqrt{x} -4x$ at the point $(1,-2)$ intersects the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\sqrt{x}-4x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{1/2}-4x) \\\\  & ={x}^{-1/2}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n     \n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm=f'(1) = (1)^{-1/2} - 4 = -3,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\nm' = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = \\dfrac 1 3.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-2) & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-1) \\\\ y+2 & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}x-\\frac{1}{3}-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}x-\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x-\\frac{7}{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(7,0).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba61258-92a9-511e-891f-9e6adce2edb0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intercepts of a Normal Line"}] :topic/math-academy-id 987 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050960}] :topic/title "Calculating the Equation of a Normal Line Using Differentiation"}]}
